    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A Hunkpapa Lakota named Jumping Badger at birth, he took his father's name, Tȟatȟáŋka Íyotake, after counting coup on a Crow warrior at 14, and as a man refused any dependence on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> government.</li><li>In 1876 he gathered a large camp of Lakota and Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheyenne">Cheyenne</a>, performed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-dance">Sun Dance</a> and had a vision of soldiers falling; the camp defeated the 7th Cavalry under Custer on 25 June 1876.</li><li>After four years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> he surrendered in 1881, toured with Buffalo Bill's Wild West, and was killed on 15 December 1890 by agency police who were sent to arrest him because officials feared he would join the Ghost Dance.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-dance">Sun Dance</a> of 1876, Sitting Bull described a vision of soldiers, &quot;as thick as grasshoppers&quot;, falling upside down into the Lakota camp; about three weeks later the camp defeated Custer's 7th Cavalry.</p></aside>
    </section>

